From: Timofey Maykov <itnitro@gmail.com>
Subject: dts: be7000: do not vote the MP5496 l2 regulator over RPM

The l2 node came from the IPQ9574 RDP boards, where l2 is the 1.8 V
supply of the USB PHY PLLs. With it the kernel sends an RPM request for
l2 at boot. The stock firmware never votes any RPM rail. On part of the
boards that request kills the receive side of the UNIPHY0 <-> QCA8084
10G-QXGMII lane: the SoC gets no block lock, PRBS31 from the chip is
all errors, while every UNIPHY, XPCS, SerDes and clock register reads
the same as on stock. Removing only the l2 node fixes the lane on such
a board (zerc00l, bisected: clk_ignore_unused alone and s1 >= 875 mV
alone do not help; iperf3 941 Mbit/s both ways, no lane errors).

Qualcomm says the same: l2 is the UNIPHY supply, the PMIC sets it to
850 mV from OTP and software should not change it (patchwork 14128430,
"Fix 'l2' regulator min/max voltages", never merged). The USB PHY PLLs
move to l5 at 1.8 V, as in mainline commit 4f4c905e6a2a ("Fix USB vdd
info"); l2 is not described, like on stock.
--- a/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9574-be7000.dts
+++ b/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9574-be7000.dts
@@ -307,7 +307,12 @@
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <1075000>;
 		};
 
-		mp5496_l2: l2 {
+		/* L5 feeds the USB PHY PLLs, as in mainline 4f4c905e6a2a. L2 is
+		 * the UNIPHY supply: the PMIC sets it to 850 mV from OTP and it
+		 * must not be voted (1.8 V from the RDP dtsi kills the lane to
+		 * the QCA8084 on some boards), so it is not described at all,
+		 * like stock. */
+		mp5496_l5: l5 {
 			regulator-min-microvolt = <1800000>;
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <1800000>;
 			regulator-always-on;
@@ -931,7 +936,7 @@
 };
 
 &usb_0_qmpphy {
-	vdda-pll-supply = <&mp5496_l2>;
+	vdda-pll-supply = <&mp5496_l5>;
 	vdda-phy-supply = <&regulator_fixed_0p925>;
 
 	status = "okay";
@@ -939,7 +944,7 @@
 
 &usb_0_qusbphy {
 	vdd-supply = <&regulator_fixed_0p925>;
-	vdda-pll-supply = <&mp5496_l2>;
+	vdda-pll-supply = <&mp5496_l5>;
 	vdda-phy-dpdm-supply = <&regulator_fixed_3p3>;
 
 	status = "okay";
